ceSlug": "louis-count-of-vaudmont", "sourceName": "Louis, Count of Vaudémont", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "naples-italy", "targetName": "Naples, Italy", "context": "Louis, Count of Vaudémont, died during the siege of Naples in 1528, a pivotal event in his quest for the Neapolitan throne."}, {"sourceSlug": "charles-v-holy-roman-emperor", "sourceName": "Charles V, Holy Roman Emperor", "verb": "CAUSES", "targetSlug": "louis-count-of-vaudmont", "targetName": "Louis, Count of Vaudémont", "context": "The extensive territorial claims and power of Charles V directly challenged Louis's aspirations, leading to their military confrontation over Naples."}], "places": [{"name": "Bar-le-Duc, France", "role": "Birthplace"}, {"name": "Naples, Italy", "role": "Site of death and siege"}, {"name": "Lorraine, France", "role": "Family domain"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Louis, Count of Vaudémont", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "louis-count-of-vaudémont"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q1993565", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Louis,_Count_of_Vaud%C3%A9mont"], "tags": ["France", "Religious Figures", "Europe", "comparative-religion", "doctrine-development", "cultural-diffusion"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "Louis, Count of Vaudémont is remembered as a landmark person of the Medieval era, referenced in 0 Wikipedia language editions. Key lasting impacts include: Shaped religious practice and spiritual communities; Contributed to synthesis of classical and religious thought informing Renaissance and Reformati.", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 6, "significanceNarrative": "Louis's brief but impactful life and his claim to Naples represent a significant regional episode within the larger Italian Wars, underscoring the enduring power of dynastic rights and the geopolitical struggles that shaped the early modern European balance of power. His death contributed to the consolidation of Habsburg control over southern Italy, a critical outcome for the next two centuries.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-06-21T07:53:43Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#27897729982", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Louis de Lorraine was a nobleman of Lorraine who attempted to claim the Kingdom of Naples. He was styled as the Count of Vaudémont.", "newValue": "Louis de Lorraine, Count of Vaudémont (1500–1528), was a scion of the House of Lorraine and a pivotal, albeit ultimately unsuccessful, claimant to the Neapolitan throne during the tumultuous Italian Wars. Born in Bar-le-Duc, his lineage, through his grandmother Yolande of Anjou, linked him directly to the Angevins who had formerly held the Kingdom of Naples. His brief but impactful life was thus entangled in the complex dynastic rivalries between the Valois of France and the Habsburgs of Spain, who fiercely vied for supremacy in Italy and across Europe in the early 16th century.

His most significant actions transpired during the War of the League of Cognac (1526–1530), a conflict that saw Pope Clement VII, France, Venice, Florence, and Milan arrayed against the formidable Holy Roman Emperor Charles V. Louis, leveraging his ancestral claim, joined the French forces under Odet de Foix, Vicomte de Lautrec, in the ambitious 1527–1528 expedition to conquer Naples. He led a contingent of Lorraine troops, actively participating in the siege of Naples, a critical phase of the war. His presence legitimized the French-Angevin claim in the eyes of some Neapolitan factions and demonstrated the enduring power of historical dynastic rights in Early Modern European politics.

Louis's premature death on September 2, 1528, during the siege of Naples, dealt a significant blow to the French-Angevin cause and effectively extinguished the immediate Vaudémont claim to the Neapolitan crown. His passing, amidst a devastating plague that ravaged the French camp, contributed to the eventual failure of the French expedition and the consolidation of Spanish Habsburg control over southern Italy. While his direct legacy was truncated, his actions underscored the persistent geopolitical instability of the Italian peninsula and the intricate web of alliances and rivalries that characterized the period."}, {"timestamp": "2026-06-21T07:53:43Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#27897729982", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Educated at in the 1500 – 1800 CE", "type": "Person", "year": "1500"}, {"title": "Influenced by in the 1500 – 1800 CE", "type": "Person", "year": "1500"}, {"title": "Born into in the 1500 – 1800 CE", "type": "Person", "year": "1500"}], "newValue": ["The ongoing Italian Wars created a volatile environment where dynastic claims to territories like Naples became strategic opportunities for European powers.", "Louis's direct lineage from Yolande of Anjou provided a legitimate, albeit contested, ancestral claim to the Kingdom of Naples.", "The formation of the League of Cognac in 1526 presented an alliance against Habsburg dominance, offering Louis a military and political platform to pursue his Neapolitan aspirations."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-06-21T07:53:43Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#27897729982", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Founded through Louis, Count of Vaudémont's historical significance", "type": "Person"}, {"title": "Wrote through Louis, Count of Vaudémont's historical significance", "type": "Person"}, {"title": "Established through Louis, Count of Vaudémont's historical significance", "type": "Person"}, {"title": "Transformed through Louis, Count of Vaudémont's hist